          "description": "REFEC-3: `refuelingControlTechnology.refuelingTechAdjustment`, the onboard vapour-recovery penetration T, at this bin's (process 18, model year, regulatory class, source type, fuel type, age). It is looked up on the BIN and not on the rate row because the bin relation is seven times smaller and carries the regulatory class this table needs -- conventions 72.5's rule about where a spine over a large table belongs, and `refuelingControlTechnology` is 189,379 rows."
        },
        "bin_controlledRefuelingRate": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sourcebin_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`refuelingControlTechnology.controlledRefuelingRate` at the same six keys: the displaced-vapour rate of a fully controlled vehicle."
        },
        "bin_hasRefuelingControl": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sourcebin_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Whether that table covers this bin at all. It carries fuel types 1 and 5 ONLY, so this is what makes the displacement block 64 cohorts where spillage is 104 -- a coverage fact about the table, tested on its own flag because a penetration of exactly 0 is a real value and the table is full of them (conventions 30)."
        },
        "bin_spillTechAdjustment": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sourcebin_rows"
          ],
          "description": "REFEC-5: `sourceTypeTechAdjustment.refuelingTechAdjustment` at (process 19, source type, model year). The spillage side's penetration has no fuel type and no regulatory class, which is why it cannot share the displacement side's lookup: 0 through 1995 and 0.5 from 1996 for a passenger car."
        },
        "coh_prefixSelected": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "cohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "An inclusive prefix count of the selection over the candidate grid: how many candidates at or before this one survive."
        },
        "coh_rank": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "cohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The prefix count ZEROED on non-members, so every surviving candidate has a dense 1..125 and every suppressed one has a 0 that no output ordinal can match (docs/esm-conventions.md §22). This is what keeps WHICH cohorts survive out of the document: the output row joins to this rank and reads its keys back through the join."
        },
        "bin_sourceBinActivityFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sourcebin_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The EQUIPPED source-bin activity fraction, now per BIN, which is what MOVES's `SourceBinDistribution` is a table of."
        },
        "bin_usedFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sourcebin_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S12, source_bin_distribution_generator.rs:1590-1637: the same activity re-attributed to the fuel each cohort actually BURNS. The equipped and used sides must agree on model year, engine technology, regulatory class and model-year group and differ only in the fuel. This is the column the base rate is weighted by (sbweighted.rs:148-165), and using the equipped one instead is a factor of 55 on the E85 rows. PER COUNTY, because `fuelUsageFraction` is keyed by county -- E85 is not available everywhere, so the same cohort grid is re-attributed differently in each. The self-join matches on the SOURCE TYPE as well as the model year, engine technology and regulatory class: with one source type selected that was implied, and with two a passenger car would be re-attributed a light commercial truck's activity."
        },
