          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "sulfateemissionrate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "SulfateEmissionRate.polProcessID: SO2 on one process, 3101 running exhaust through 3191 auxiliary power. The table is named for sulfate and is read by `SO2Calculator` alone.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_sulfateemissionrate",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "polProcessID"
            }
          }
        },
        "ser_fuelTypeID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "sulfateemissionrate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "SulfateEmissionRate.fuelTypeID. There is no fuel-type-9 row, and that absence is the whole of why electricity emits no SO2.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_sulfateemissionrate",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "fuelTypeID"
            }
          }
        },
        "ser_modelYearGroupID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "sulfateemissionrate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "SulfateEmissionRate.modelYearGroupID, self-describing: `SO2Calculator.sql` decodes it as floor(g/10000) and mod(g,10000).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_sulfateemissionrate",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "modelYearGroupID"
            }
          }
        },
        "ser_meanBaseRate": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "MJ/kg/ppm",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "sulfateemissionrate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "SulfateEmissionRate.meanBaseRate. Declared MJ/kg per ppm because that is what cancels the sulfur level it multiplies and the energy content the formula divides by: the chain ratio it builds is grams of SO2 per unit of energy, which this document -- like MOVES -- carries as dimensionless.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_sulfateemissionrate",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "meanBaseRate"
            }
          }
        },
        "ser_firstModelYearID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sulfateemissionrate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The first model year of `ser_modelYearGroupID`."
        },
        "ser_lastModelYearID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "sulfateemissionrate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The last model year of the same identifier."
        },
        "pol_pollutantID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "pollutant_rows"
          ],
          "description": "pollutant.pollutantID.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_pollutant",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "pollutantID"
            }
          }
        },
        "pol_globalWarmingPotential": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "pollutant_rows"
          ],
          "description": "pollutant.globalWarmingPotential: 1 for atmospheric CO2, 28 for methane, 265 for nitrous oxide, NULL for the other 113. `CO2AERunningStartExtendedIdleCalculator` step 2 reads it for its three inputs.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_pollutant",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "globalWarmingPotential"
            }
          }
        },
        "iss_pollutantID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "integratedspeciesset_rows"
          ],
          "description": "integratedSpeciesSet.pollutantID: a species NonHAPTOG is TOG WITHOUT. The national table is fourteen rows and one set -- mechanism 5, `NonHAPTOG Mechanism`, set 4. The set column is not read; a mechanism with two sets would need it.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_integratedspeciesset",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "pollutantID"
            }
          }
        },
        "iss_mechanismID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "integratedspeciesset_rows"
          ],
          "description": "integratedSpeciesSet.mechanismID.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_integratedspeciesset",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "mechanismID"
            }
          }
        },
        "iss_mechanismPollutantID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "integratedspeciesset_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The PSEUDO-POLLUTANT that selects this mechanism: 1000 + 500 x (mechanismID - 1), which is 3000 for mechanism 5. `TOGSpeciationCalculator.java` decodes it the other way, `1 + (pollutantID - 1000) / 500`. A run selects a mechanism by selecting that pollutant on a process -- `chain-so2-co2e-mechanism` selects 300001 -- and NOT by selecting NonHAPTOG: `chain-nonhaptog` selects 8801 and no mechanism, and MOVES emits no NonHAPTOG for it."
        },
        "zmh_temperature": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "degF",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "zonemonthhour_rows"
          ],
          "description": "zonemonthhour.temperature, 59.5 degF at hour 7. The temperature adjustment reads THIS column and the A/C activity term and the EV suppression read `zmh_heatIndex`; the two are equal on the 17 of this snapshot's 24 hours that are below 78 degF -- hour 7 among them -- and differ by up to 1.6 degF on the other seven, so the distinction costs nothing at THIS hour and is made because adjust.rs:495-535 makes it.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_zonemonthhour",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "temperature"
            }
          }
        },
        "zmh_relHumidity": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "percent",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "zonemonthhour_rows"
          ],
          "description": "zonemonthhour.relHumidity, in PERCENT and not as a fraction -- 90.400001525879 at hour 7. It is the second argument of the heat-index regression and it is read for no other purpose here; below 78 degF the regression is not evaluated at all, so on this fixture's own hour the column is ingested and then not used, which is exactly why `zmh_heatIndex` is asserted over hours the branch DOES reach.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "db_zonemonthhour",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "relHumidity"
            }
          }
        },
        "rspp_ordinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The pollutant-process relation's own row ordinal, so the rate and output relations join to it by position."
        },
        "rspp_pollutantID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The pollutant half of the packed identifier: 91, 107, 117."
        },
        "rspp_processID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The process half: 1 (Running Exhaust) and 10 (Tirewear)."
        },
        "rspp_occursOnRealRoads": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Whether this pollutant-process's rates live on a real road, looked up from `emissionprocess` by its process half. 1 on all three here."
        },
        "rspp_hasBaseRateTableRow": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does EITHER zero-hour rate table carry a row at this pollutant-process -- anywhere, for any source bin? It is a fact about the DATABASE and not about the run's cohorts, which is why it is asked of the table and not of the rate relation: it decides which road type the pollutant-process's rows live on, and a road type cannot depend on which vehicles a RunSpec happened to select."
        },
        "rspp_isSilentlyRequired": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Is this pollutant-process a chain PREREQUISITE the RunSpec never asked for? `ExecutionRunSpec.flagRequiredPollutantProcesses` adds Total Energy Consumption at running, start and extended-idle exhaust whenever a refueling loss is selected, because without it `BaseRate` emits no energy and the chained calculator sees no input; the addition is flagged `silentlyRequired` and the output processor strips it. So `process-refueling` computes 250 energy rows and emits none of them.\n\nIT IS DERIVED FROM `runspecpollutantprocess` AND THE DECLARED NEEDS TABLE, not read: the execution database records the EXPANDED set and not the user's. That is a real limit, and it has exactly one blind spot -- `require()` does not flag a pair the user had already selected, so a RunSpec asking for refueling AND for Total Energy Consumption would keep its energy block and this document would drop it. No run in the corpus does that, and the needs table is refueling-only, which is what makes the rest derivable."
        },
        "rspp_rateRoadTypeID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The road type this pollutant-process's rows live on. `BaseRateGenerator` writes a real-road process's rates on the run's on-network road type and an off-network one's (start exhaust, extended idle, auxiliary power) on road type 1 alone (`rspp_ratedRoadTypeID`). A pollutant-process with no row in either rate table is not `BaseRateGenerator`'s at all -- it is a chained calculator's, stamped on its PARENT's row -- so it takes its parents' road type, closed over the chain in three rungs (`rspp_chainRoadTypeID1..3`): crankcase extended idle (17) is chained to extended idle (90) and lives on road type 1 with it, while a refueling loss, whose start and extended-idle energy parents are on no selected road in a run that selects road type 4 alone, lives on 4 with its running-energy parent. Until the off-network processes arrived every chained process took the run's road type, and every one still does that has an on-network parent."
        },
        "rspp_isEmitted": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Is this pollutant-process one of the output's blocks? It has to happen on a selected road type, have a rate, and be a SELECTION rather than a prerequisite the chain closure pulled in. The first two are measured on `expand-criteria`: eight are selected, four pass the road-type test and three are emitted. The third is `process-refueling`'s, and only its."
        },
        "rspp_occursOnSelectedRoad": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does this pollutant-process happen on a road type the RunSpec selected? This is what `rspp_isEmitted` used to be, whole."
        },
        "rspp_hasRatedRow": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does any rate row of this pollutant-process have a RATE? The same rule the cohort count obeys, on the other axis: on `expand-criteria` four pollutant-processes pass the road-type test and three are emitted, because SO2 running exhaust is selected, happens on a road, and has no rate row at all."
        },
        "rspp_emittedPrefix": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "An inclusive prefix count of the emission mask over the pollutant-process relation: how many at or before this one are emitted. The same construction `coh_prefixSelected` uses one axis down (docs/esm-conventions.md 22)."
        },
        "rspp_outputOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which output block this pollutant-process owns, numbered 1..n_outputPolProcess over the emitted ones and 0 for a discarded one -- an ordinal no output row can match. 1, 2, 3 here."
        },
        "rspp_usesAvgSpeedModes": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which generator wrote this pollutant-process's `RatesOpModeDistribution`, and therefore which operating-mode family its weights live on. It is a PROCESS test because the reference makes it one on both sides: `BaseRateGenerator` drives the drive cycles itself only for processes 1 and 9 (mod.rs:156-161) and `AverageSpeedOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` handles process 10 and errors out for anything else (avg_speed_op_mode_distribution.rs:110-116, and its executeLoop logs 'AvgSpeedOMDG called for unknown process'). Both constants are ported as enum members rather than as digits. 0 on the 9101 row and 1 on both tire-wear rows."
        },
        "rspp_usesEvapModes": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Whether this pollutant-process takes its operating-mode weights from the EVAPORATIVE distribution rather than from either RatesOpModeDistribution. `EvaporativeEmissionsOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` subscribes to the evaporative processes and writes a distribution over soaking and running rather than over speed bins, so the two families are alternatives and not a sum. `EvaporativeEmissionsOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` writes it for permeation (11), fuel vapour venting (12) and fuel leaks (13), and so does this: the three differ in what they multiply the distribution by, not in the distribution."
        },
        "rspp_isEnergy": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Whether S18's kilojoule-to-MillionBTU conversion applies. engine.rs:1286-1310 applies it to pollutants 91, 92 and 93 and to nothing else, so the two tire-wear pollutants stay in grams. The membership rule is lib/onroad_activity.esm's `is_energy_pollutant` rather than three inline comparisons, and 92 and 93 are named there even though this run selects neither."
        },
        "rspp_unitDivisor": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The divisor S18 applies after the group-by sum: 1,055,055.9 kJ per Million BTU for an energy pollutant and exactly 1 for a mass one."
        },
        "ppmy_shortModYrGroupID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "pollutantprocessmodelyear_rows"
          ],
          "description": "modelyeargroup.shortModYrGroupID resolved AT the pollutantprocessmodelyear row. It has to be here rather than on the cohort because a join key column must be one-dimensional, and the cohort's short group depends on the pollutant-process as well: 9101 maps model year 2020 to short group 40 (a two-digit year) and 11710 maps every model year to 0, modelYearGroup 0's \"Doesn't Matter\". Same cohort, different source bin -- and a third encoding beside brake wear's decade bands."
        },
        "rt_ordinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The rate relation's own row ordinal. The relation is the n_runspecpollutantprocess pollutant-processes crossed with the n_agecategory x n_runspecsourcefueltype cohort candidate grid -- 3 x 164 = 492 rows, every factor a DISCOVERED extent. It exists because everything the base rate needs is keyed by BOTH, and a join key column has to be one-dimensional (docs/esm-conventions.md 2: tables stay tables)."
        },
        "rt_polProcOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which pollutant-process this rate row is for. Pollutant-process major, so each one occupies a contiguous run of 164 rows."
        },
        "rt_outputBlockOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The output block this rate row's pollutant-process owns, read back from `rspp_outputOrdinal`. 0 on every row of a discarded pollutant-process, which is what keeps `rt_outputOrdinal` from matching anything."
        },
        "rt_binOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which source-bin candidate this rate row is for. The rate relation is the pollutant-process crossed with the BIN and not with the cohort, because every rate lookup below it keys on a regulatory class, an engine technology or both: `emissionrate` on all five bin columns, `temperatureadjustment` and `evefficiency` on the regulatory class, `fleetavgadjustment` on the group it belongs to."
        },
        "rt_cohortOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "And which cohort that bin belongs to -- the key the output rank is built on, and the one the key columns and the activity join use."
        },
        "rt_polProcessID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The pollutant-process this rate row carries."
        },
        "rt_pollutantID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its pollutant."
        },
        "rt_processID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its process."
        },
        "rt_unitDivisor": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its output-unit divisor."
        },
        "rt_modelYearID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The cohort's model year."
        },
        "rt_yearOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The rate row's cohort's year ordinal: what every per-year quantity the rate reads -- the fuel supply, the evaporative weights, the activity -- is joined to it by."
        },
        "rt_fuelTypeID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The cohort's fuel type."
        },
        "rt_engTechID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The bin's engine technology."
        },
        "rt_regClassID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The bin's regulatory class."
        },
        "rt_regClassNotRequired": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Whether `sourceTypePolProcess` says this rate row's (source type, pollutant-process) bins WITHOUT a regulatory class. `SourceBinDistributionGenerator` then writes the bin with regClassID 0 (source_bin_distribution_generator.rs:1355), and the rate tables are keyed by that bin, so the rate joins must be too. A pair with no `sourceTypePolProcess` row keeps its class: absence is not 'N'."
        },
        "rt_rateRegClassID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The regulatory class the rate joins read -- C2' of docs/evap-permeation.md 0.1. The bin's own class, or 0 where the process does not require one. It is ONLY the rate lookup's key: nothing downstream is keyed by it, because PC-1b puts the class back by weight and the output carries none. The failure it prevents is the bad kind -- a copied spine keeps every row and multiplies every one of them by a rate of zero, and `process-evap-permeation`'s 64 rows came out exactly 0 before it."
        },
        "rt_sourceTypeID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The rate row's source type, read off its cohort candidate. It replaces the run-level scalar every one of these joins used to reach for."
        },
        "rt_sourceTypeOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The same source type as a rank into `runspecsourcetype_rows`: the key that reads the activity relation and the electric-vehicle temperature clamp back onto this rate row. It read the drive cycle's operating-mode distribution too until 7d-next, when that became per vehicle and `rt_physicsOrdinal` took it over."
        },
        "rt_physicsOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which VEHICLE this rate row's emissions are driven by: the `sourceusetypephysics` row whose source type and regulatory class are the bin's and whose model-year range contains the bin's model year, as its rank on the drive-cycle axis. This is aggregate.rs:351-360 exactly -- a regulatory-class equality and a model-year CONTAINMENT, which is why the second is a filter and the first a join. A rate row that matched no vehicle would weight its operating modes by nothing and emit 0, so `run_rateRowsWithoutPhysics` counts them."
        },
        "rt_ageID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The cohort's age in the analysis year."
        },
        "rt_ageGroupID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The cohort's age GROUP, which is the EV-efficiency key."
        },
        "rt_stmyFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The bin's sample-vehicle fraction."
        },
        "rt_fleetAvgGroupID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The cohort's fleet-average group."
        },
        "rt_evFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
