        "rtDay_meanBaseRate": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S14 (J26): the operating mode is contracted away against the weights of THIS RATE ROW'S OWN pollutant-process, reached through J41. This is `baserate_1_2020.meanBaseRate` on the energy rows and `baserate_10_2020.meanBaseRate` on the tire-wear ones, computed rather than read -- and the two are collapsed against DIFFERENT weight vectors: 23 drive-cycle modes for 9101 and 16 speed-bin modes for 11710. The two rate tables are seven orders of magnitude apart, 392,141 kJ per source-hour against 0.0000197 g, so a wrong weight is a wrong number in every cell. Per county, through the same rebase. THE WEIGHTS ARE THIS RATE ROW'S OWN VEHICLE'S as of 7d-next, reached through `rt_physicsOrdinal` rather than through the source type: a light commercial truck's regulatory class 30 and its regulatory class 41 have different fixed mass factors, so their drive-schedule seconds bin into different operating modes and the same rate table collapses to two different numbers. A permeation row is the exception: `EvaporativePermeationCalculator` sums the rate over its operating modes and weights the TEMPERATURE ADJUSTMENT by the distribution instead (PC-1b, PC-2b), so its weight is `rtDay_permeationWeight`, which carries no mode. A fuel-vapour-venting row's mode-300 rate is also multiplied by TVV-8's `tempAdj x rvpAdj` (`rt_ventingAdjustment`). Its mode-151 rate is the cold-soak insert's, TVV-2 to TVV-7, which this document does NOT compute: it is the only thing that reads the venting chain, and on an on-network link it is weighted by exactly 0 (docs/evap-fvv.md 0.3). A START row's weight is the start operating-mode distribution, and its mode rate carries StartTempAdjustment's additive term, which the Go adds to the weighted rate before anything else touches it -- so the criteria ratio scales it too."
        },
        "rtDay_meanBaseRateACAdj": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The same contraction of the incremental A/C load: `baserate_*.meanBaseRateACAdj`, under the same weights -- the hotelling ones on a hotelling row, which the Go scales by hourFractionAdjust too. Per county, likewise."
        },
