        },
        "rt_hasTogChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Presence of the TOG arm. It has no table, so this is the pollutant test alone -- and what stops TOG being emitted where its parents are not is `rt_parentEmitsHere`, which is the other half of `rt_emits` and not this."
        },
        "rt_pm10ChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J36, PM10BrakeTireCalculator.sql: PM10PM25Ratio for the CHAINED pollutant-process, on this cohort's fuel type and source type, in the model-year range that brackets its model year (lib/keys.esm's `model_year_in_range`). Zero on the two unchained rate paths, because `pm10emissionratio` carries polProcessID 10710 and nothing else -- so the chain term vanishes there with no branch. 6.6667 on every chained row here, which is why docs/process-tirewear.md 7.2 says this fixture cannot see the fuel key or the range."
        },
        "rt_noxChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J79, NOCalculator and NO2Calculator: `NONO2Ratio` for the chained SPECIES -- NO, NO2 or HONO -- on this cohort's fuel type and source type, at the model-year group its own pollutant-process maps this model year to. Zero everywhere else, including on total NOx itself, which is the parent and not a species. The join has no electricity row to find, so it is also what makes a species block 104 cohorts where its parent is 124."
        },
        "rt_crankcaseChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J80, CrankcaseEmissionCalculator: `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` for the chained crankcase process on this cohort's fuel type, source type and regulatory class, in the model-year range that brackets its model year. Zero everywhere else."
        },
        "rt_chainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The ratio a chained pollutant-process scales EACH of its parents by. Eleven arms (particulate, NOx species, crankcase, methane, NMHC, NMOG and VOC, TOG, the air toxics, SO2, CO2 and CO2 equivalent), disjoint by pollutant-process, so the union is a sum. It gained the county and the month with the hydrocarbon species: their tables are keyed by the fuel SUBTYPE, which a rate row reaches only through the county's fuel supply and its formulations, and the E10 correction is a criteria ratio, which was already per county and month."
        },
        "rt_hasPm10ChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does `PM10EmissionRatio` carry a row for this pollutant-process, fuel type, source type and model year? A PRESENCE test, separate from the value, because a ratio of exactly zero is a ratio."
        },
        "rt_hasNoxChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does `NONO2Ratio` carry a row for this species, fuel type, source type and model-year group? Zero for every electricity row, which is the whole of why a species block is 104 cohorts and its parent 124."
        },
        "rt_hasCrankcaseChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` carry a row for this crankcase process, fuel type, source type, regulatory class and model year? It does for diesel model years 2001 and later, and the ratio there is exactly 0.0 -- a cohort that is emitted as a zero, not a cohort that is absent."
        },
        "rt_isSulfatePMSpecies": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "1 on a rate row whose POLLUTANT `crankcaseEmissionRatio` also carries on an exhaust process -- which is how the table itself says `SulfatePMCalculator` owns it. The crankcase lift does not claim such a row. Without this, `runspecs/pending/process-pm-exhaust.esm` emitted PM sulfate as ALL of NonECPM, 1.7x to 68x high, and PM water as NonECPM where MOVES has 0.0. Derived from the table and not listed, because the list IS the table's exhaust-process rows and `CalculatorInfo.txt` agrees with it: `CrankcaseEmissionCalculatorNonPM` registers only gaseous pollutants, and the PM crankcase variant is superseded by `SulfatePMCalculator` and registers nothing."
        },
        "rt_hasChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Is this rate row chained from something, with a ratio recorded for it in any of the eleven arms? The union is a `max` because the arms are disjoint and a presence flag is not additive. It stays a function of the rate row alone while the ratio itself is per county and month: whether a table HAS a row for a cohort does not depend on which county is asking."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsGen1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 1 of the chain's presence fixed point: does a pollutant-process this row is CHAINED FROM carry a RATE of its own on this same cohort? The rate relation self-joined THROUGH `runspecchainedto`, under `max` because the cohort's source bins would otherwise be counted one per bin -- and through the EDGE LIST rather than through a derived parent key, because TOG has two parents and a key column can hold one. THE CHAIN IS A FIXED POINT, UNROLLED THREE TIMES. A chained pollutant-process can itself be the parent of another: `chain-tog-speciation` runs THC (101) to NMHC (7901) to NMOG (8001) to TOG (8601), and `process-airtoxics` runs THC to NMHC to VOC (8701) to benzene (2001). Reading the parent's DIRECT quantity, which is what one pass does, gives a grandchild zero. Three is not a guess: it is the longest chain in the whole snapshot corpus (docs/esm-conventions.md §71), measured over every `runspecchainedto` in `../moves.rs/characterization/snapshots`, and a run deeper than three would leave its deepest species at zero rather than wrong -- `run_chainDeeperThanThree` counts exactly those rows and is asserted at 0. The three passes are spelled out rather than factored because a `faq`'s ranges and join clause are equation-level and an expression template cannot carry them; what IS shared -- the emission predicate -- is lib/emission_factors.esm's `chained_emits`, applied at all three."
        },
        "rt_emitsGen1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`rt_emits` as one pass of the chain would compute it: a row emits if it has a rate of its own, or if its ratio exists and its parent has a rate of its own. Correct for every chain one deep, which is every RunSpec in `runspecs/` before the hydrocarbon species."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsGen2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 2: does the parent emit at generation 1? The same self-join, one rung further from the root."
        },
        "rt_emitsGen2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`rt_emits` after two passes: correct for every chain two deep."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsHere": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 3, the last: does the pollutant-process this row is CHAINED FROM emit after two passes? A species is emitted where its ratio exists AND its parent was emitted; for the particulate chains the two are the same set, for the nitrogen ones the ratio is the narrower test, and for the hydrocarbon species the parent is itself a species. THE CHAIN IS A FIXED POINT, UNROLLED THREE TIMES. A chained pollutant-process can itself be the parent of another: `chain-tog-speciation` runs THC (101) to NMHC (7901) to NMOG (8001) to TOG (8601), and `process-airtoxics` runs THC to NMHC to VOC (8701) to benzene (2001). Reading the parent's DIRECT quantity, which is what one pass does, gives a grandchild zero. Three is not a guess: it is the longest chain in the whole snapshot corpus (docs/esm-conventions.md §71), measured over every `runspecchainedto` in `../moves.rs/characterization/snapshots`, and a run deeper than three would leave its deepest species at zero rather than wrong -- `run_chainDeeperThanThree` counts exactly those rows and is asserted at 0. The three passes are spelled out rather than factored because a `faq`'s ranges and join clause are equation-level and an expression template cannot carry them; what IS shared -- the emission predicate -- is lib/emission_factors.esm's `chained_emits`, applied at all three."
        },
