        {
          "id": "the_two_operating_mode_families_are_two_binnings_of_one_activity",
          "description": "W, checked on the properties that do not require the reference, for BOTH mode families. It is a DISTRIBUTION on either: the weights sum to the avgSpeedFraction column's own total, which is 1 to that column's six stored significant figures and not exactly, so the gate is ABSOLUTE at 1e-5 and not relative (docs/esm-conventions.md 20.5). That the drive-cycle family and the tire-wear family reach the SAME total is what says the second is a re-binning of the same activity and not a second one -- if the tire-wear arm had missed a speed bin its total would be short by that bin's fraction and nothing else in this document would notice.\n\nTHE MODE COUNTS ARE THE TIRE-WEAR-SPECIFIC ASSERTION HERE. 23 on the 9101 rows, the drive-cycle set. 16 -- NOT 17 -- on both tire-wear rows. Operating mode 400, `tirewear;idle`, is in `operatingmode`, is associated with 11710 by `opmodepolprocassoc`, and carries 31 `emissionrate` rows, so a reader may reasonably expect a 17-wide vector. No speed bin names it: `avgspeedbin.opModeIDTirewear` runs 401 to 416, and 400 is reachable only through `buildOpModeClause`'s `when linkAvgSpeed < 0.1 then 400`, which is the PROJECT-domain branch (avg_speed_op_mode_distribution.rs:118-125). This run is Inventory scale. `pp_tireIdleWeight` says the same thing as a number rather than as a count. This is tire wear's mode 501 -- docs/process-tirewear.md 3.2 has the measurement, and unlike brake wear's it also matters that mode 400's rate is exactly 0.",
